1
00:00:00,192 --> 00:00:03,323
Claude's interface is really user-friendly.

2
00:00:03,724 --> 00:00:05,923
Let me still guide you through the core

3
00:00:06,052 --> 00:00:09,311
ideas behind this interface. At the center

4
00:00:09,327 --> 00:00:12,361
of our screen, we have the chat input

5
00:00:12,553 --> 00:00:15,363
window. There we can implement our tasks

6
00:00:15,604 --> 00:00:17,418
that we want Claude to fulfill.

7
00:00:18,252 --> 00:00:20,853
With the plus symbol, we can add more

8
00:00:21,046 --> 00:00:24,305
options, more complexity to our tasks with

9
00:00:24,353 --> 00:00:27,210
concepts like skills and connectors. Which

10
00:00:27,306 --> 00:00:28,733
we'll explore in this course.

11
00:00:29,567 --> 00:00:31,940
The most important setting here though is

12
00:00:32,036 --> 00:00:34,586
the selection of the model, the AI model

13
00:00:34,698 --> 00:00:35,676
that should be used.

14
00:00:36,302 --> 00:00:39,092
Sonnet is the balanced default model,

15
00:00:39,493 --> 00:00:42,571
capable enough for most everyday cowork

16
00:00:42,619 --> 00:00:45,858
tasks while staying reasonably fast.

17
00:00:46,484 --> 00:00:50,076
Anthropic also offers Haiku as the fastest

18
00:00:50,092 --> 00:00:53,315
and cheapest tier and Opus as the most

19
00:00:53,363 --> 00:00:54,116
capable model,

20
00:00:54,742 --> 00:00:57,082
especially in combination with extended

21
00:00:57,371 --> 00:00:57,675
thinking.

22
00:00:58,589 --> 00:01:02,068
This is the setup for complex, high-stakes

23
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:04,761
tasks actually, but as mentioned, it

24
00:01:04,761 --> 00:01:08,704
consumes more tokens. In the left sidebar

25
00:01:08,769 --> 00:01:11,317
here, we can dive deeper into Cowork.

26
00:01:11,879 --> 00:01:13,962
Well, obviously we can also switch to Chat

27
00:01:14,139 --> 00:01:16,624
and Code, but we'll stick to Cowork in this

28
00:01:16,624 --> 00:01:16,928
course.

29
00:01:17,585 --> 00:01:19,990
At the top, you have this magnifier search

30
00:01:20,134 --> 00:01:23,073
option, which allows you to explore previous

31
00:01:23,346 --> 00:01:26,462
chats, code sessions, or cowork sessions you

32
00:01:26,478 --> 00:01:29,482
had. You can find the chat symbol here for

33
00:01:29,482 --> 00:01:30,911
the chats and the

34
00:01:31,538 --> 00:01:34,172
cowork symbol for previous cowork sessions.

35
00:01:35,152 --> 00:01:37,465
New tasks will always start a new window

36
00:01:37,481 --> 00:01:37,690
here,

37
00:01:38,284 --> 00:01:41,256
and projects, scheduled, customize, and

38
00:01:41,288 --> 00:01:41,722
dispatch

39
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:44,757
are the options we'll explore a bit later in

40
00:01:44,806 --> 00:01:45,255
this course.

41
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:48,323
At the bottom, we can access our user

42
00:01:48,323 --> 00:01:50,585
account. In here, you can upgrade to a

43
00:01:50,585 --> 00:01:53,037
different plan in case the selected plan is

44
00:01:53,069 --> 00:01:54,079
not sufficient for you,

45
00:01:54,736 --> 00:01:56,724
and you can also explore the settings.

46
00:01:57,654 --> 00:02:00,138
Some areas in the settings should be set

47
00:02:00,203 --> 00:02:00,732
correctly.

48
00:02:01,309 --> 00:02:04,290
For example, in the privacy section, we

49
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:07,384
should make sure that location metadata and

50
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:10,446
help improve Claude are disabled just to

51
00:02:10,446 --> 00:02:12,594
keep our data a bit more private.

52
00:02:13,732 --> 00:02:16,876
In the capabilities menu, We should activate

53
00:02:16,876 --> 00:02:19,669
search and reference chats and generate

54
00:02:19,669 --> 00:02:22,255
memory from chat history. This makes it a

55
00:02:22,255 --> 00:02:25,594
lot easier for Claude Cowork to keep things

56
00:02:25,691 --> 00:02:28,677
organized, to remember context and previous

57
00:02:28,677 --> 00:02:29,255
information.

58
00:02:30,267 --> 00:02:32,740
If we scroll down further to the code

59
00:02:32,836 --> 00:02:34,971
execution and file creation

60
00:02:35,517 --> 00:02:36,031
section,

61
00:02:36,705 --> 00:02:39,483
both options here should also be activated

62
00:02:39,676 --> 00:02:42,485
because Cowork might write some code in the

63
00:02:42,485 --> 00:02:45,470
background to fulfill your tasks. Therefore,

64
00:02:45,855 --> 00:02:47,749
these settings should be activated as

65
00:02:47,797 --> 00:02:50,445
mentioned too. Finally, we go to the Cowork

66
00:02:50,445 --> 00:02:53,735
section and there global instructions can be

67
00:02:53,735 --> 00:02:53,991
set.

68
00:02:55,083 --> 00:02:57,955
This is a persistent prompt which is applied

69
00:02:57,955 --> 00:02:59,624
to every Cowork session

70
00:03:00,282 --> 00:03:03,556
where you can define a role, a tone, rules,

71
00:03:03,893 --> 00:03:06,605
output preferences, anything like that. So

72
00:03:06,621 --> 00:03:09,093
if you have any settings, any core rules

73
00:03:09,125 --> 00:03:11,662
that should apply to every Cowork sessions,

74
00:03:12,016 --> 00:03:14,312
you can set it in here. One instruction you

75
00:03:14,344 --> 00:03:17,427
could add is always ask before deleting or

76
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:18,647
overwriting files.

77
00:03:19,482 --> 00:03:22,132
By default, Cowork has an integrated

78
00:03:22,132 --> 00:03:24,990
security check, so it should always ask you

79
00:03:25,087 --> 00:03:27,849
before it is deleting files. Still, you can

80
00:03:27,849 --> 00:03:30,739
explicitly state that again, kind of as a

81
00:03:30,884 --> 00:03:32,313
double security mechanism.

82
00:03:33,196 --> 00:03:35,251
So this is the interface and the core

83
00:03:35,299 --> 00:03:37,965
settings. This means we can now dive into

84
00:03:37,965 --> 00:03:39,890
our first practical project here.
